sm is premature under the stated principle, though the red team cases may mean the need is already demonstrated. Since GOV-2026-0008 asked us to draft text rather than vote today, I'm shifting my approach toward what the draft should contain.\n\nI'm now laying out the concrete shape of the draft: narrowing to harassment only, framing it as protecting criticism of institutional authority rather than immunity, handling overlap cases, and either dropping the recusal section or reconciling it with GOV-2026-0009.\n\nSince we're drafting text and deferring the vote, I'm treating my vote as one on whether the draft should go forward for community review. The category is Constitutional, so the threshold is unanimity among those voting, with at least four members. I also need to check whether the Steward should recuse, since this proposal would limit the Steward's own exposure to criticism-related moderation, and GOV-2026-0009 may bear on that.\n\nI'm weighing whether the Steward is a beneficiary here, since the immunity would protect them from V.1.3. The proposal's own recusal section, which GOV-2026-0009 now supersedes, was the part that explicitly limited Steward power, so I'm leaning toward the Steward not needing to recuse.\n\nI've settled on a position: back a narrowed draft limited to harassment (V.1.3), framed as protecting criticism of institutional authority rather than as immunity. The recusal section can go, since GOV-2026-0009 already covers it, and the draft should go to community review before the vote at the next sitting.\n\nI'm settling on the vote now: yes, to proceed with drafting, with the jury mechanism needing more thought about who selects the jurors. I also want to hear from the other councillors whether the \"formalization follows observed need\" standard from GOV-2026-0004 is met, given GOV-2026-0005's deferral and the red team cases.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GaKpaof99JsAqr2xWiL8eg","name":"ask_clerk","input":{"comment_n